# Suggests and not Recommends: the game is complete without it, and pulling
# PySide6 onto every machine that wanted an arcade game would be rude. But
# nothing pointed at the trainer at all before this, so a game-only install had
# no way to find out the other half exists — apt and the software centres show
# Suggests, and the game's own TRAIN AI entry is the other half of the answer.
Suggests:
 missile-defense-trainer
Description: faithful Atari Missile Command clone (Qt 6 + Vulkan)
 A modern, faithful clone of Atari's Missile Command (1980), built on a
 deterministic C++ simulation and rendered with Qt 6 and Vulkan.
 .
 Defend six cities from descending waves of ICBMs, splitting MIRVs and
 blast-dodging smart bombs by launching interceptors from three ammo-limited
 batteries. It features procedurally generated retro sound effects and music,
 a persistent high-score table with arcade initials entry, an options screen,
 and fullscreen support.
 .
 The audio backend (ALSA or PulseAudio) is loaded at runtime if present; the
 game runs silently if neither is available, hence the Recommends.
 .
 This package is the game alone and depends on no Python. Training your own
 agent is the separate missile-defense-trainer package.

Package: missile-defense-trainer
Build-Profiles: <!pkg.missile-defense.nopython>
# Not `games`, which it inherits from the source stanza: lintian rejects a
# games-section package that ships no game, and it is right to — this is a
# training workbench with charts in it. `science` is where Debian files the
# machine-learning tooling it sits beside.
Section: science
# `any`, not `all`: this package carries `_md_native`, a compiled extension. It
# was `all` while the binding lived in a `python3-md` of its own, and that split
# is gone — two packages for two products, and the Python half is not a product
# anyone was installing on purpose.
#
# What that costs, stated rather than discovered later: `apt install python3-md`
# for the environment alone, on a headless box with no Qt, is no longer possible.
# Anyone who wants that has `pip install missile-defense`, which is the same code
# and the audience it was really for. And Debian Python Policy would rather a
# module in `dist-packages` came from a `python3-*` package than from this one;
# that is a deliberate trade for having two names instead of three.
Architecture: any
# python3-venv is not decoration. The trainer's answer to "this machine has no
# PyTorch" is to build one itself — `python3 -m venv` into the per-user data
# directory, then pip from an allow-listed index (runs/runtime.py). Debian ships
# `venv` in a package of its own and `${python3:Depends}` does not pull it, so
# without this the one-time setup step fails on its first command, on a machine
# that had done nothing wrong. It is a Depends and not a Recommends because
# installing a training runtime is the reason this package exists.
Depends:
 ${python3:Depends},
 ${misc:Depends},
 ${shlibs:Depends},
 python3-numpy,
 python3-pyside6.qtcharts,
 python3-pyside6.qtwidgets,
 python3-venv
Recommends:
 missile-defense
Suggests:
 python3-torch,
 python3-pynvml
Description: trainer for the Missile Defense learned agent
 A desktop application for training, watching and comparing learned Missile
 Defense policies: it starts and stops runs, plots the score against the scripted
 baseline live, lists the episodes a run recorded, and opens them in the game.
 .
 The window never needs PyTorch itself — it starts each training run as a
 separate process, so a run survives the window being closed or crashing, and it
 can install a managed training runtime for you if this machine has no PyTorch.
 That is why python3-torch is only Suggested.
 .
 Installing this pulls in PySide6 (LGPL-3), which is why it is a package of its
 own: the game is MIT and depends on none of it.
